\openserialport opens a serial port, for example to
communicate with an Arduino.
It takes two arguments, the port and the baud rate.
The port can be either its number or its path, as printed
by \listserialports:

\listserialports
\openserialport 0 115200

or

\openserialport /dev/ttyACM0 115200

\listserialports must be called first, as the port is
looked up in the list made by that command. If the number
is out of range or the path is not in the list, you will
get an error.
Only one serial port can be open at a time.

Once open, data can be sent with \serialwrite and
\serialprint, and the port can be closed with
\closeserialport.
